00:03:37,641 --> 00:03:42,040
most advanced tunnel wash systems in the industry.

51
00:03:42,041 --> 00:03:46,580
Despite its size and cutting edge technology, Cloyster's tunnel system was built like most

52
00:03:46,581 --> 00:03:50,980
others from the ground up.

53
00:03:50,981 --> 00:03:56,520
A standard tunnel wash starts with a 4 by 150 foot long pit.

54
00:03:56,521 --> 00:04:01,400
Concrete lines the pit and creates the car wash foundation.

55
00:04:01,401 --> 00:04:06,360
The pit has a 30 degree slope to the side and is 6 and a half feet deep on one end and

56
00:04:06,361 --> 00:04:07,320
two and a half on the other.

57
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:11,700
This allows a trough to carry water and sludge away for drainage.

58
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:16,260
A pipe at the end of the trough attaches to holding tanks outside.

59
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:19,900
Encased electrical wiring runs the length of the pit.

60
00:04:20,980 --> 00:04:22,180
A tunnel covers the pit.

61
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:26,680
And finally, a conveyor pushes the cars through the wash.

62
00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:29,960
Wash and soap arches are ready to do the work.

63
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:32,460
Now, let's wash some cars.

64
00:04:32,461 --> 00:04:36,880
When we bring the vehicle in, we load it onto a loading belt.

65
00:04:36,881 --> 00:04:38,960
And that's much easier for the customer.

66
00:04:38,961 --> 00:04:42,140
That way they don't have to put their tire into a small conveyor track.

67
00:04:42,141 --> 00:04:44,220
We'll take care of that mechanically for them.

68
00:04:44,221 --> 00:04:48,740
We'll spray it down first with an alkaline material that helps to start the pre-cleaning.

69
00:04:48,741 --> 00:04:51,860
And it'll also help to break down any bugs that are on the vehicle.

70
00:04:51,861 --> 00:04:54,940
Next, it is treated with high-pressure rinsing.

71
00:04:54,941 --> 00:05:00,320
That is used primarily to clean any hard-to-clean areas and the areas around the windshield.

72
00:05:00,321 --> 00:05:02,660
and it also helps remove the alkaline cleaner

73
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:05,060
that we've already put on.

74
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:09,560
As the car comes down, it will self-load into the conveyor

75
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:11,860
by means of the correlator and a slug

76
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:14,300
that will allow the car to move freely.

77
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:17,840
It'll push the vehicle without any harm to the wheel or the tire

78
00:05:17,940 --> 00:05:18,800
onto the conveyor.

79
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:26,120
In the 1950s, conveyors helped automate the car wash industry.

80
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:28,620
And one of the largest and oldest names in the business

81
00:05:28,621 --> 00:05:33,260
This is the Jim Coleman Company, located in Houston, Texas.

82
00:05:33,261 --> 00:05:37,840
Today it's part of the Coleman Hanna Company, and they produce approximately a hundred different

83
00:05:37,841 --> 00:05:39,440
automated conveyor systems.

84
00:05:39,441 --> 00:05:43,480
This is a standard, heavy-duty car wash conveyor.

85
00:05:43,481 --> 00:05:50,500
It's manufactured with heavy-duty steel, with rollers, chain, and a hydraulic motor.

86
00:05:50,501 --> 00:05:56,400
When a computer senses the car is in the right position, a roller will pop up, and it takes

87
00:05:56,401 --> 00:06:01,742
about one horsepower to be able to push a vehicle through the car wash.

88
00:06:01,743 --> 00:06:02,720
This conveyor

89
00:06:02,721 --> 00:06:08,195
will push the car all the way through the wash, rinse, wax, and drying process.

90
00:06:08,196 --> 00:06:08,896
We have

91
00:06:08,897 --> 00:06:13,294
this plastic rail here to protect the outside of the tire.

92
00:06:13,295 --> 00:06:15,680
When the car exits the tunnel,

93
00:06:15,681 --> 00:06:24,100
this roller here will go below the surface to be reused again.

94
00:06:24,101 --> 00:06:27,200
the conveyor first pushes vehicles through an optical sensor,

95
00:06:27,201 --> 00:06:29,600
which signals the computer to start the wash cycle.

96
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:34,440
As the vehicle comes down, the soap is applied,

97
00:06:34,441 --> 00:06:36,520
and it goes into what's called a emitter.

98
00:06:36,521 --> 00:06:39,280
This is a cloth that is scratch-free,

99
00:06:39,281 --> 00:06:41,120
and what it does is create enough friction

100
00:06:41,121 --> 00:06:43,380
along with the soap to clean the surface.

101
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:46,720
Next piece of equipment is a wrap around.

102
00:06:46,721 --> 00:06:48,320
Again, it's a felt-like material

103
00:06:48,321 --> 00:06:50,500
that's used to clean the front, side,

104
00:06:50,501 --> 00:06:52,320
and rear surfaces of the vehicle.

105
00:06:54,100 --> 00:07:01,480
The Hannah Coleman Company makes their brushes out of both cloth and foam, each designed

106
00:07:01,481 --> 00:07:03,180
for a specific purpose.

107
00:07:03,181 --> 00:07:08,160
This is where all the brush manufacturing starts right here.

108
00:07:08,161 --> 00:07:09,860
We buy rolls of material.

109
00:07:09,861 --> 00:07:11,540
This machine cuts the material.

110
00:07:11,541 --> 00:07:17,140
We use a cloth material that we call a textile that absorbs water and it provides very good

111
00:07:17,141 --> 00:07:19,540
cleaning results on the surface of the car.

112
00:07:19,541 --> 00:07:23,060
We cut it into small fingers to be able to wash the car effectively.

113
00:07:23,061 --> 00:07:29,220
We also have available a foam material that uses more water and more chemicals, but it's

114
00:07:29,221 --> 00:07:34,380
a closed cell foam that's softer and more gentle to the surface of the vehicle.

115
00:07:34,381 --> 00:07:41,500
Manufacturers have designed different sized brushes to cover every inch of a car's exterior.

116
00:07:41,501 --> 00:07:46,200
This material is an extra wide cut, so when it drags across the surface of the vehicle,

117
00:07:46,201 --> 00:07:51,640
it provides gentle cleaning ability, whereas this material is for the sides of the vehicle

118
00:07:51,641 --> 00:08:00,640
It's a very lightweight material, and it's designed to clean under mirrors, around mirrors, door handles, and stuff like that around license plate frames.

119
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:07,720
Workers sew the brushes to plastic liners, which are then inserted into an aluminum brush core.

120
00:08:07,721 --> 00:08:09,736
You slide it into the segments.

121
00:08:09,737 --> 00:08:15,980
This brush core is spinning inside of a car wash where it is washing the surface of the vehicle.

122
00:08:15,981 --> 00:08:23,940
And once they're in action, the brushes will last for about 150,000 washes before they need to be replaced.

123
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:31,700
Next comes a dousing of polish from above and then some scrubbing down below.

124
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:41,960
And what this white wall brush will do is spin hydraulically and air will engage it to push it into the raised white letters or the white walls of this vehicle.

125
00:08:41,961 --> 00:08:44,600
It's a nylon material, nylon bristles.

126
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:46,500
It'll engage into the tire,

127
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:49,200
cleaning the raised white letters or the white walls.

128
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:52,560
A second series of brushes removes the suds

129
00:08:52,561 --> 00:08:53,740
in preparation for rinsing.

130
00:08:56,900 --> 00:08:59,740
A high-pressure nozzle rinses off most of the soap

131
00:08:59,741 --> 00:09:02,280
while the wheels are under some pressure of their own.

132
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:05,380
This is a wheel cleaner.

133
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,520
It uses high pressure with zero-degree nozzles

134
00:09:08,521 --> 00:09:11,460
to apply high pressure directly to the rim of the vehicle,

135
00:09:11,461 --> 00:09:12,263
cleaning it.

136
00:09:12,264 --> 00:09:17,680
The wheel blaster is hydraulically driven and the computer will keep track of the

137
00:09:17,681 --> 00:09:23,280
position of the wheel in relationship to the length of the body of the car so it knows where

138
00:09:23,281 --> 00:09:25,474
the wheel is on every vehicle.

139
00:09:25,475 --> 00:09:30,300
And finally, a rain bar drops a high volume of water onto the car,

140
00:09:30,820 --> 00:09:35,400
rinsing any last residue from the vehicle before it enters the first of two drying rooms.

141
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:38,080
Total water used per vehicle?

142
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:40,200
43 gallons.

143
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:43,200
Some car washers use more, some less,

144
00:09:43,420 --> 00:09:46,680
but that's still less than we use to take an average bath.

145
00:09:49,180 --> 00:09:53,860
Today, America's car washes use 1.3 million gallons of water per year,

146
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:57,900
making the need for a more eco-friendly system that uses less water

147
00:09:57,901 --> 00:09:59,460
and puts out less waste.

148
00:10:00,660 --> 00:10:04,580
Many, like the Cloister Car Wash, rely on a recycled water system.

149
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:11,140
And it all starts here with the water main that comes in from the city.

150
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:17,160
And this water is pumped up and placed in these holding tanks to be used out on the line as needed.

151
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:22,120
This is the hydraulic pack that drives everything hydraulically driven on the conveyor.

152
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:28,160
These are the high-pressure pumps and motors that drive all of the high-pressure water out to the conveyor itself.

153
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:31,040
These are the chemicals in the different pumping units

154
00:10:31,041 --> 00:10:32,980
that will mix and blend the chemical

155
00:10:32,981 --> 00:10:36,220
and send it out to the conveyor to properly wash the vehicle.

156
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:38,080
These are the water softeners.

157
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:42,000
We soften the water because soft water will adhere better to the soap

158
00:10:42,001 --> 00:10:43,660
and allow it to lather better.

159
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:48,480
The hard water pumped in from the city contains calcium and magnesium,

160
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,760
minerals that don't bind well with soap.

161
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:54,160
The water softener removes them.

162
00:10:54,161 --> 00:11:01,420
But where does all the grimy, polluted water go after each wash?

163
00:11:01,421 --> 00:11:04,680
That would be in the water trough, under the conveyor.

164
00:11:04,681 --> 00:11:07,380
The conveyor pit is at its deepest point here.

165
00:11:07,381 --> 00:11:11,280
All of the water that is generated will flow into this deepest portion, and as you can

166
00:11:11,281 --> 00:11:15,680
see there's a metal grate over the pipes that would stop any large solids.

167
00:11:15,681 --> 00:11:20,460
This is designed this way to flush any solids down to this particular area where it will

168
00:11:20,461 --> 00:11:25,800
go out separation tanks and the solids will be separated from the water.

169
00:11:25,801 --> 00:11:31,260
A six-inch pipe transports the sludge water to a 2,000-gallon sediment tank, where about

170
00:11:31,261 --> 00:11:34,720
80% of the solids sink to the bottom.

171
00:11:34,721 --> 00:11:42,560
The water continues on to a second tank, where another 15% of the waste is separated out.

172
00:11:42,561 --> 00:11:44,560
The water then enters a final tank.

173
00:11:44,561 --> 00:11:48,940
There, a third of it is filtered and piped back into the car wash to be reused.

174
00:11:50,740 --> 00:11:53,340
The remaining water flows out into the sewer pipes.

175
00:11:54,740 --> 00:11:57,420
The captured solid waste will be vacuumed out for disposal.

176
00:11:59,380 --> 00:12:03,960
Thanks to the Federal Clean Water Act, operators must monitor water pollutants.

177
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:10,660
If they exceed acceptable levels, the water must be treated before it can be released into the sewer system.

178
00:12:10,661 --> 00:12:17,660
After the final rinse, the car enters the drying rooms.

179
00:12:17,661 --> 00:12:22,660
The first drying room shoots air onto the vehicle to push the water off the surface.

180
00:12:22,661 --> 00:12:28,660
Each blower produces 54,000 cubic feet per minute of airflow, or the equivalent of 1,800

181
00:12:28,661 --> 00:12:31,660
home hair dryers.

182
00:12:31,661 --> 00:12:36,660
The car travels into a second drying room through a set of airtight doors.

183
00:12:36,661 --> 00:12:39,660
We closed the doors in order to keep the ambient temperature

184
00:12:39,661 --> 00:12:42,660
of the room high enough to effectively evaporate the water

185
00:12:42,661 --> 00:12:43,660
off the surface.

186
00:12:45,660 --> 00:12:48,660
Then the Cloister car wash experience continues with a drive

187
00:12:48,661 --> 00:12:50,660
