As you can see here, we've got the dung pretty much out of the way.

488
00:29:14,540 --> 00:29:16,060
The bird poop, we're going to discard to that.

489
00:29:17,320 --> 00:29:19,580
After Victor has removed the lubricated dung,

490
00:29:19,960 --> 00:29:22,160
he clears the leftovers with a cleaner wax.

491
00:29:23,020 --> 00:29:27,220
Made of synthetic polishes and waxes, it removes any markings left from the dung.

492
00:29:29,480 --> 00:29:34,740
Next, a thorough wash and a good waxing leaves it bright and shiny.

493
00:29:40,820 --> 00:29:44,560
Victor can return the Porsche to the unsuspecting owner better than before.

494
00:29:49,180 --> 00:29:52,020
This is the Clive, Utah Disposal Facility.

495
00:29:52,160 --> 00:29:55,260
one of several federally funded sites across the U.S.

496
00:29:56,420 --> 00:30:00,640
where low-level radioactive waste from excavated soil is sent to die.

497
00:30:02,900 --> 00:30:05,380
Specialized train cars hold the contaminated earth

498
00:30:05,381 --> 00:30:08,000
from a variety of locations scattered around the U.S.

499
00:30:09,160 --> 00:30:11,040
It's up to workers to remove it,

500
00:30:12,440 --> 00:30:13,260
store it,

501
00:30:13,860 --> 00:30:19,520
and then clean the rail cars so that they can return for another pickup.

502
00:30:19,521 --> 00:30:23,020
Welcome to the nuclear car wash.

503
00:30:26,360 --> 00:30:29,160
These rail cars, they're basically filled with soils

504
00:30:29,161 --> 00:30:32,000
that were contaminated during the research and development

505
00:30:32,001 --> 00:30:33,960
of nuclear weapons during the Cold War.

506
00:30:34,860 --> 00:30:36,860
Before these rail cars can be cleaned,

507
00:30:37,260 --> 00:30:39,200
the radioactive waste has to be removed.

508
00:30:40,440 --> 00:30:42,740
During the winter months, the rail cars travel

509
00:30:42,741 --> 00:30:44,020
through sub-zero temperatures,

510
00:30:44,400 --> 00:30:47,500
which freezes the contaminated soil into blocks of ice.

511
00:30:47,501 --> 00:30:53,820
So when they arrive, a locomotive pushes the cars into the specially designed thaw room.

512
00:30:53,821 --> 00:30:58,900
Waste comes in and it's frozen to a point where it cannot be dumped out of the rail car.

513
00:30:58,901 --> 00:31:04,300
We developed this thaw building to remove the ice from the soils which enable it to be dumped.

514
00:31:04,301 --> 00:31:11,860
This thaw building consists of 84 high infrared heaters that heat the car from both the sides and the bottom of the rail car.

515
00:31:11,861 --> 00:31:13,320
to give you an idea.

516
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:15,940
Two of these heaters that heat the sides of the car

517
00:31:15,941 --> 00:31:18,320
could actually heat a 2,500-square-foot home.

518
00:31:18,980 --> 00:31:20,720
It takes about 48 minutes

519
00:31:20,721 --> 00:31:23,180
to go all the way down through this process.

520
00:31:25,200 --> 00:31:27,260
Once the radioactive soil is thawed,

521
00:31:27,720 --> 00:31:30,040
the car is rolled into a gigantic cylinder,

522
00:31:30,880 --> 00:31:31,720
the rotary dump.

523
00:31:33,340 --> 00:31:34,820
The top is lifted off,

524
00:31:36,080 --> 00:31:39,400
then a 75-horsepower motor drives a set of gears,

525
00:31:39,401 --> 00:31:43,640
which flipped the quarter million pound train car on its side.

526
00:31:48,640 --> 00:31:51,740
The wrench ring has 80,000 pounds of counterweight

527
00:31:51,741 --> 00:31:52,740
on the top of the ring.

528
00:31:53,580 --> 00:31:56,120
That weight balances out the weight of the rail car,

529
00:31:56,420 --> 00:31:59,620
which allows the 75 horsepower motor to turn the car over.

530
00:32:03,260 --> 00:32:06,320
Now it's time to begin the first stage of the rail car washing.

531
00:32:06,321 --> 00:32:10,320
workers unleash high-pressure water cannons

532
00:32:10,321 --> 00:32:12,320
on the car's interior.

533
00:32:12,321 --> 00:32:15,320
The water cannon operates at 300 gallons a minute

534
00:32:15,321 --> 00:32:17,320
and 90 psi.

535
00:32:17,321 --> 00:32:20,320
It gives us the ability to clean the radioactivity

536
00:32:20,321 --> 00:32:22,320
and soil from the rail car.

537
00:32:22,321 --> 00:32:24,320
As you'll notice, all it takes is water.

538
00:32:24,321 --> 00:32:27,320
It doesn't take any chemicals to release that radioactivity.

539
00:32:27,321 --> 00:32:30,320
We're not trying to break down the radioactivity.

540
00:32:30,321 --> 00:32:33,320
We're just trying to wash it out and clean the rail car

541
00:32:33,321 --> 00:32:35,320
for future use.

542
00:32:35,321 --> 00:32:45,320
An underground pipe system carries the water used to clean the train car to a lined holding

543
00:32:45,321 --> 00:32:50,940
pond.

544
00:32:50,941 --> 00:32:55,660
As for the radioactive dirt, trucks haul it to an isolated area on the compound where

545
00:32:55,661 --> 00:33:01,840
it will sit for a hundred years while it decays.

546
00:33:01,841 --> 00:33:06,100
The inside water blasted clean, the outside of the train car is now ready to be washed.

547
00:33:08,600 --> 00:33:14,220
Similar to the car wash, the train is transported on a conveyor, and all of the cleaning functions are activated.

548
00:33:15,320 --> 00:33:17,660
The automatic wash is triggered by the photo eye.

549
00:33:18,020 --> 00:33:22,060
When the exterior of the vehicle crosses this photo eye here, the wash system will activate,

550
00:33:22,480 --> 00:33:23,800
and the chemical arch will start up.

551
00:33:24,620 --> 00:33:29,060
It connects itself to the indexer, which is our conveyor for the vehicle through the automatic wash.

552
00:33:29,061 --> 00:33:36,360
The chemical is applied in an efficient manner by determining how much water and soap it

553
00:33:36,361 --> 00:33:40,820
takes to cover the surface as it goes by at one foot per second.

554
00:33:40,821 --> 00:33:45,740
This space between the chemical arch and the spinner arch is designed to allow the chemical

555
00:33:45,741 --> 00:33:48,880
the time it takes to clean the soils off of the surface of the vehicle.

556
00:33:48,881 --> 00:33:53,280
The spinner arch is going to apply high pressure, high volume water to the surface of the vehicle

557
00:33:53,281 --> 00:33:57,660
to efficiently clean the vehicle for all of the contaminated surfaces.

558
00:33:57,661 --> 00:33:59,900
The blowers are going to come on after the spinners,

559
00:33:59,901 --> 00:34:03,100
and the blowers are going to blow the water off.

560
00:34:03,101 --> 00:34:05,060
Finally, the conveyor moves the rail car

561
00:34:05,061 --> 00:34:08,060
through radiation detectors.

562
00:34:08,061 --> 00:34:10,200
If the detectors register a level safe enough

563
00:34:10,201 --> 00:34:13,320
to transport food, the train car exits the building

564
00:34:13,321 --> 00:34:14,900
and hits the rail for another pickup.

565
00:34:18,180 --> 00:34:20,580
The stakes aren't quite as high for this industrial wash

566
00:34:20,581 --> 00:34:25,640
system, although a truck's lifespan depends on it.

567
00:34:25,641 --> 00:34:28,860
It's a tire and undercarriage wash built by Stanton Systems

568
00:34:28,861 --> 00:34:33,900
to clean the trucks that dump salt on the highway during a snowstorm to hasten melting.

569
00:34:35,400 --> 00:34:39,280
Salt can literally destroy a truck's underside, so getting it off is crucial.

570
00:34:39,780 --> 00:34:42,740
This is a salt truck just in after salting.

571
00:34:43,340 --> 00:34:46,680
As you can see, it's loaded with salt all over the place,

572
00:34:46,681 --> 00:34:49,800
under the vehicle, up through all the equipment,

573
00:34:50,720 --> 00:34:53,460
corroding it, eating it, destroying the truck.

574
00:34:53,461 --> 00:34:55,120
This is the plow.

575
00:34:55,540 --> 00:34:57,800
As you can see, the salt's all over it, too.

576
00:34:59,720 --> 00:35:01,500
The wash design is simple enough.

577
00:35:02,880 --> 00:35:06,320
A 2,400-gallon water tank sits below the wash.

578
00:35:07,540 --> 00:35:11,680
A pump sends water from the tank through its 70 jets that line the wash area.

579
00:35:13,080 --> 00:35:15,360
After the jets rinse wash off the salt,

580
00:35:15,820 --> 00:35:18,860
the water falls through grates and into a sloped catch basin.

581
00:35:20,160 --> 00:35:21,820
Solids move to the bottom of the basin

582
00:35:21,821 --> 00:35:24,480
while pipes recycle the water back to the water tank.

583
00:35:25,360 --> 00:35:27,280
The water contains no soap.

584
00:35:28,620 --> 00:35:33,900
It has a chemical in it to buffer out the corrosive nature of the salt.

585
00:35:34,640 --> 00:35:37,300
The pressure of the wash is around 60 psi

586
00:35:37,301 --> 00:35:40,120
and about 600 gallons a minute,

587
00:35:40,720 --> 00:35:43,780
enough to wash salt off the moving vehicle.

588
00:35:45,200 --> 00:35:47,920
That's a lot of water, but it's all recycled.

589
00:35:48,680 --> 00:35:50,560
When it turns milky white with salt,

590
00:35:51,140 --> 00:35:54,660
Usually after a couple of heavy snowstorms, the water is replaced.

591
00:35:56,100 --> 00:36:00,140
As the truck goes in, the plow is washed first.

592
00:36:01,020 --> 00:36:02,560
The sides of the trucks are washed.

593
00:36:02,680 --> 00:36:04,500
The undercarriage then is washed.

594
00:36:04,780 --> 00:36:08,360
All the equipment on the side of the truck, the underneath of the truck,

595
00:36:08,361 --> 00:36:14,520
all the harnesses, the I-beams, and all the hardware on back of the truck are washed.

596
00:36:16,580 --> 00:36:18,960
Stanton also makes portable systems.

597
00:36:18,961 --> 00:36:21,680
that can be up and running in less than a day.

598
00:36:23,380 --> 00:36:26,280
Of course, most of us don't need an industrial wash.

599
00:36:28,320 --> 00:36:30,460
But would you believe car manufacturers

600
00:36:30,461 --> 00:36:33,120
are designing cars with a car wash in mind?

601
00:36:41,600 --> 00:36:44,340
Modern cars aren't just designed to weather the elements.

602
00:36:45,940 --> 00:36:48,400
They're also designed to survive the car wash.

603
00:36:48,960 --> 00:36:57,760
That means manufacturers like Hyundai in Montgomery, Alabama, have to secure a car's every nook

604
00:36:57,761 --> 00:37:01,820
and cranny.

605
00:37:01,821 --> 00:37:07,200
And that starts inside the paint shop, where robotic arms coat cars with four layers of

606
00:37:07,201 --> 00:37:15,520
paint, a primer, two color coats, and a final acrylic layer called the clear coat.

607
00:37:15,521 --> 00:37:19,680
And that's really there to protect against scratching and marring.

608
00:37:19,980 --> 00:37:22,380
The wire brushes, the cloth brushes,

609
00:37:22,700 --> 00:37:26,000
any mechanism that rolls against the surface of the car,

610
00:37:26,380 --> 00:37:27,860
that's what the clear coat protects.

611
00:37:29,180 --> 00:37:32,000
Next, workers start closing up any gaps.

612
00:37:32,520 --> 00:37:33,760
Take the sunroof, for example.

613
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:43,060
The two elements in a car wash that we're trying to control is air and water.

614
00:37:43,061 --> 00:37:50,320
And so the critical feature of a sunroof to control air and water from entering the cabin is the seal.

615
00:37:50,540 --> 00:37:52,420
You can see the seal along the glass.

616
00:37:52,860 --> 00:37:59,280
It meets up to the body, and that prevents water and air from intruding into the cabin while we're moving through the car wash.

617
00:38:00,860 --> 00:38:04,380
Hyundai designers have built a gutter around the sunroof to collect water.

618
00:38:05,840 --> 00:38:07,540
Plastic tubing takes it from there.

619
00:38:07,541 --> 00:38:18,460
The tubing that you see is designed to carry the water away from the sunroof, out of the gutter,

620
00:38:18,461 --> 00:38:21,600
and out through the body as it's threaded through the body,

621
00:38:22,380 --> 00:38:26,800
and out to the natural environment.

622
00:38:28,180 --> 00:38:32,120
There was a time when automakers used to put wipers high up on the windshield.

623
00:38:32,121 --> 00:38:40,340
Today we try to design the windshield wipers so they're tucked underneath the surface of

624
00:38:40,341 --> 00:38:46,460
the hood so any of the brushes or the mechanisms in a car wash don't have an opportunity to grab

625
00:38:46,461 --> 00:38:50,613
the wiper, yank it off the glass, or destroy the structure of the wiper.

626
00:38:50,614 --> 00:38:51,940
So when we put the hood

627
00:38:51,941 --> 00:38:57,780
down, that's where you can really see that this surface sits above the top of the windshield,

628
00:38:57,781 --> 00:39:03,440
so when the wiper blades come or the washing blades come, it can't catch below the wiper.

629
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:08,540
Then there's that graded area between the top of the hood and the base of the windshield.

630
00:39:09,360 --> 00:39:14,180
That's the air intake for the heating and air conditioning systems and a potential water collection area.

631
00:39:15,360 --> 00:39:17,422
The water runs down the windshield.

632
00:39:17,423 --> 00:39:20,780
It'll go through the grill feature in the cover cowl top.

633
00:39:21,120 --> 00:39:26,120
So the cowl inner is designed to carry the water away from the air conditioning and heater opening

634
00:39:26,121 --> 00:39:28,980
so that we don't suck water into those systems.

635
00:39:28,981 --> 00:39:32,160
So we have a drain design that has an opening

636
00:39:32,161 --> 00:39:34,360
on the right-hand side and left-hand side.

637
00:39:34,361 --> 00:39:37,260
The water flows through there, out onto the ground,

638
00:39:37,261 --> 00:39:40,260
and not into the cabin where the customer is.

639
00:39:40,261 --> 00:39:43,280
That is specifically designed to protect

640
00:39:43,281 --> 00:39:45,280
against the car wash environment.

641
00:39:47,680 --> 00:39:49,680
Remember those steel bumpers that protruded

642
00:39:49,681 --> 00:39:51,580
beyond the body frame?

643
00:39:51,581 --> 00:39:54,080
Not good inside a car wash.

644
00:39:54,081 --> 00:39:59,360
The brushes in the car wash on the older cars could get caught in that bumper system.

645
00:39:59,361 --> 00:40:05,260
So today, you have a plastic, what we call a fascia, that covers the actual bumper structure.

646
00:40:05,460 --> 00:40:10,660
So there aren't any exposed corners or exposed features that a brush could catch.

647
00:40:11,800 --> 00:40:15,100
And let's not forget these old inflexible side mirrors.

648
00:40:15,980 --> 00:40:18,700
The spinning side brushes could break them right off the car.

649
00:40:20,920 --> 00:40:23,900
Structure of the mirror is absolutely something that we consider.

650
00:40:24,080 --> 00:40:26,840
and we designed for in the car wash environment.

651
00:40:27,240 --> 00:40:29,000
We have three connection points.

652
00:40:29,240 --> 00:40:31,400
This provides a three-point base.

653
00:40:31,500 --> 00:40:33,340
That's the strongest base that you can have.

654
00:40:33,380 --> 00:40:36,120
You always want to have kind of a tripod-type design.

655
00:40:36,680 --> 00:40:41,020
And then on the exterior, we have a molded-in plastic base and housing

656
00:40:41,021 --> 00:40:44,140
that covers the actual physical structure of the mirror.

657
00:40:44,141 --> 00:40:47,280
But both of these elements are designed so that it won't fracture,

658
00:40:47,540 --> 00:40:51,240
it won't move, won't vibrate, won't cause any problems in the car wash.

659
00:40:51,241 --> 00:40:55,240
The underbelly has its own set of car wash obstacles.

660
00:40:55,241 --> 00:41:01,240
With the high pressure spray, any of the components under the vehicle, especially wire harnesses

661
00:41:01,241 --> 00:41:04,240
like we have here, you want to make sure they're watertight.

662
00:41:04,241 --> 00:41:08,240
These brake lines, you can see they're tucked in underneath the structure.

663
00:41:08,241 --> 00:41:11,240
Then we have a plastic protector to cover them as well.

664
00:41:11,241 --> 00:41:15,240
We have plugs that come in here. We have a sealant that goes under the body.

665
00:41:15,241 --> 00:41:20,240
Those are all designed to protect against conveyor systems, high pressure spray,

666
00:41:20,241 --> 00:41:22,760
make sure everything's watertight and protected.

667
00:41:24,640 --> 00:41:26,200
Once everything is assembled,

668
00:41:27,080 --> 00:41:30,240
Hyundai tests their vehicles inside their very own car wash.

669
00:41:32,200 --> 00:41:33,980
The water pressure is intense,

670
00:41:34,620 --> 00:41:37,200
nearly three times that of a typical car wash.

671
00:41:37,880 --> 00:41:40,720
The shower test is where theory meets practice.

672
00:41:41,420 --> 00:41:45,200
There's 400 high-pressure heads that spray water on the vehicle,

673
00:41:45,201 --> 00:41:47,340
and we want to make sure that everything is sealed,

674
00:41:47,520 --> 00:41:48,880
there's no electrical shortages.

675
00:41:48,881 --> 00:41:52,360
all the features that we design into the vehicle

676
00:41:52,361 --> 00:41:54,440
to protect against the car wash environment,

677
00:41:54,820 --> 00:41:58,040
this is where we judge and make sure it's running the way we want it.

678
00:41:59,480 --> 00:42:01,500
After a thorough dry glove inspection,

679
00:42:02,040 --> 00:42:03,560
the vehicle gets the green light.

680
00:42:04,120 --> 00:42:04,820
Good to go.

681
00:42:07,060 --> 00:42:11,020
It's now safe to enter that modern institution called the car wash.

682
00:42:12,680 --> 00:42:16,100
It may not make your car go faster or get you better mileage,

683
00:42:16,101 --> 00:42:20,200
But if cleanliness is indeed next to godliness,

684
00:42:21,200 --> 00:42:22,860
then all hail the car wash.

685
00:42:24,000 --> 00:42:24,900
Car ready!

